    <div class="text-body text-base sm:text-[17px] leading-relaxed space-y-4 [&_h2]:text-ink [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:sm:text-xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-tight [&_h2]:mt-8 [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:leading-tight [&_h3]:text-ink [&_h3]:text-base [&_h3]:sm:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:tracking-tight [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_h3]:leading-tight [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-2 [&_a]:decoration-link/30 [&_a]:transition-colors [&_a:hover]:decoration-link">

        <p>Gmail and Outlook both let you paste an image directly into the body of a message with Ctrl+V. Most of the time, that is exactly what you want. But a pasted image and an actual attachment are not the same thing. There are several common situations where only a real file will do. Here is the difference, and how to get from one to the other.</p>

        <h2>Why a Pasted Image Is Not Always Enough</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li><strong>Plain-text email:</strong> If the recipient's client renders your message as plain text, or you switch your own compose window to plain text, the inline image is stripped out entirely. It is not shown as an attachment either.</li>
            <li><strong>File upload fields:</strong> A support ticket form, a job application, or any <code>&lt;input type="file"&gt;</code> field on a website cannot accept a paste. It only accepts a real file selected from your device.</li>
            <li><strong>Forwarding and archiving:</strong> Some mail clients handle inline images inconsistently when a message is forwarded or exported. A proper attachment, by contrast, travels with the message reliably.</li>
            <li><strong>Explicit attachment requests:</strong> If someone asks you to "attach the screenshot," a pasted inline image technically is not that. It just looks similar on screen.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Fix: Convert the Clipboard Image Into a File First</h2>
        <p>The clipboard holds image data, not a file, until something writes it to disk (see <a href="/blog/where-do-clipboard-images-go/">where clipboard images and screenshots actually go</a> for the full explanation). Pasting directly into an email body works because the mail client happens to accept that image data inline. A file upload field or an attachment button generally does not have that same ability. The workaround is simple. Paste the image into <a href="https://pastedownloadapp.com/">PasteDownloadApp</a> and download it as a PNG or JPEG. You now have an actual file on your device, and any attachment button, upload field, or drag-and-drop area will accept it.</p>

        <h2>Attaching It in Gmail</h2>
        <p>Once you have a downloaded file, open Gmail and start a new message. Click the paperclip icon in the compose toolbar, or drag the downloaded file directly into the compose window. Either method attaches it as a proper file rather than an inline image. It shows up as a separate download for the recipient, instead of embedded content in the message.</p>

        <h2>Attaching It in Outlook</h2>
        <p>In both Outlook desktop and the web version, use <strong>Insert &gt; Attach File</strong> and browse to the file you downloaded. You can also drag it into the message window the same way. If you need it to appear inline as well as attached, most versions of Outlook let you do both: paste the image into the body first, then attach the file separately.</p>

        <h2>Email Signatures Are a Special Case</h2>
        <p>Signature editors in Gmail and Outlook generally do accept a pasted image directly. A signature is stored as part of the account settings, not composed fresh each time, which is why the paste works there. If your signature editor rejects a paste or shows a broken image, there is a more reliable path: save the clipboard image as a file first, then use the signature editor's own "insert image" or upload option.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Why does my pasted screenshot show up as a tiny broken icon instead of a picture?</h3>
        <p>This usually happens when the mail client expected a file reference but received raw clipboard data it could not resolve. It often shows up after a message is saved as a draft and reopened. Downloading the image as a file and attaching it directly avoids the issue entirely.</p>

        <h3>Does this work the same way in webmail clients other than Gmail and Outlook?</h3>
        <p>Most modern webmail clients, including Yahoo Mail and iCloud Mail, support pasting an image inline in the same way. The distinction between an inline paste and a true attachment still applies. So the same fix, downloading the image first, works everywhere.</p>

        <h3>Will converting the image to JPEG instead of PNG make the email smaller?</h3>
        <p>Usually yes, for photographic screenshots with lots of color detail. PNG stays smaller for screenshots that are mostly flat UI and text, though. PasteDownloadApp lets you choose the format before downloading, so you can pick whichever produces a smaller attachment for your image.</p>

